research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-6125-kind: theorem
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-6126-title: "Hessenberg: $\\kappa \\otimes \\kappa = \\kappa$ for every infinite cardinal $\\kappa$, proved in ZF from the canonical well-order of $\\kappa \\times \\kappa$"
--
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-6400-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-6401-**No power set is involved.** [[thm-hartogs]] builds $\aleph(A)$ from the well-ordered subsets of $A$, so the successor cardinal is obtained without ever forming $\mathcal{P}(A)$ as a size. That separation is what makes the aleph hierarchy a ZF construction while the beth hierarchy is not.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-6402-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-6403-**The notation collides with the ordinal successor, and this page keeps them apart.** $\alpha^{+} = \alpha \cup \{\alpha\}$ is the **ordinal** successor ([[def-ordinal]]) and is almost never a cardinal: an infinite cardinal is a limit ordinal, so $\kappa \cup \{\kappa\}$ is not one. The cardinal successor is $\aleph(\kappa)$, which is much larger. Where an item on this page writes the superscript on an ordinal letter, as [[lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set]] does with $\alpha^{+}$, it carries the published ordinal meaning $\alpha \cup \{\alpha\}$; the cardinal successor is always written $\aleph(\kappa)$ on this page.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-6404----
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log:6405:id: cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-6406-kind: corollary
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-6407-title: "The clauses at $0$, at a successor and at a limit determine exactly one operation $\\alpha \\mapsto \\aleph_\\alpha$, in ZF, and — assuming the Axiom of Choice — exactly one operation $\\alpha \\mapsto \\beth_\\alpha$; each value is an infinite cardinal, each is strictly increasing and continuous at limits, and $\\alpha \\le \\aleph_\\alpha$"
