research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-7821-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-7822-The claim is plausible because the alephs a reader meets first are regular:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-7823-$\aleph_0$ is regular in ZF, and assuming the Axiom of Choice every **successor**
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-7824-aleph is regular ([[thm-regularity-of-the-alephs]]). It fails at the first aleph
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-7825-whose index is a limit ordinal, and the failure is a theorem of ZF requiring no
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-7826-choice principle at all.
