research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8115-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8116-**The finite summand does not vanish for a trivial reason.** $5 \sqcup \omega$ does have more elements than $\omega$ in the naive sense: it carries a tagged copy of $\omega$ and five further points. The equality $5 \oplus \aleph_0 = \aleph_0$ says only that the two sets are equinumerous, and what makes that true is that an infinite well-ordered set absorbs finitely many extra points, the same shift that makes an infinite cardinal a limit ordinal in [[thm-cardinal-arithmetic-agrees-with-finite-counting]].
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8117-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8118-**Order does not matter here, and that is not automatic.** $\oplus$ is commutative, so $5 \oplus \aleph_0$ and $\aleph_0 \oplus 5$ are the same cardinal. The ordinal $+$ on the very same objects is **not** commutative, which is precisely why [[def-cardinal-arithmetic]] gives the cardinal operation its own symbol rather than reusing $+$.
