research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8463-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8464-## Facts & Assumptions
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8465-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8466-**Given:** The Axiom of Countable Choice. Write $\omega_1$ for the first uncountable ordinal ([[def-first-uncountable-ordinal]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8467-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log:8468:[L1] $\aleph_\lambda = \bigcup\{\aleph_\beta : \beta \in \lambda\}$ at a limit ordinal $\lambda$; every $\aleph_\alpha$ is an infinite cardinal; the operation is strictly increasing; $\aleph_1 = \omega_1$ ([[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]], [[cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8469-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8470-[L2] $\omega_1$ is uncountable, is a cardinal, is a limit ordinal, and every ordinal in it is at most countable ([[thm-omega-one-is-the-least-uncountable-ordinal]], [[def-limit-ordinal]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8471-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log:8472:[L3] $\aleph_1 = \aleph_0^{+}$ is the least cardinal strictly above $\aleph_0$ ([[lem-successor-cardinal-exists]], [[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8473-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8474-[L4] Assuming $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$, every at most countable $A \subseteq \omega_1$ is bounded below $\omega_1$: $\sup A = \bigcup A$ lies in $\omega_1$ and dominates every member of $A$ ([[thm-countable-subsets-of-omega-one-are-bounded]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8475-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8476-[L5] A nonempty set is at most countable if and only if it is a surjective image of $\mathbb{N}$ ([[lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n]], [[def-countable]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8477-
--
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8497-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8498-## Remarks
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8499-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8500-**Why countable choice appears, and where exactly.** It is used once, at [L4]: without it, $\omega_1$ can consistently be the supremum of an $\omega$-indexed family of countable ordinals, and then the argument of step 1.2 collapses. That dependence is inherited, not introduced here — the published boundedness theorem carries the same hypothesis, and states so in its own title.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8501-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log:8502:**What "singular" does and does not mean.** Singular says only $\operatorname{cf}(\kappa) \ne \kappa$. The singular cardinal computed in [[ex-the-cofinality-of-aleph-omega]] has countable cofinality, and a reader who meets only that example may take the two conditions to be the same. They are not: here the cofinality is $\aleph_1$, uncountable, while the cardinal is still singular because $\aleph_1$ is far below $\aleph_{\omega_1}$.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8503-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8504-**The pattern behind both computations.** For a limit ordinal $\lambda$ the family $\alpha \mapsto \aleph_\alpha$ restricted to $\lambda$ is cofinal in $\aleph_\lambda$, so $\operatorname{cf}(\aleph_\lambda) \le \lvert \lambda\rvert$ always. The work is entirely in the lower bound, and it is a statement about $\lambda$ rather than about $\aleph_\lambda$: it asks how short a family can be and still reach $\lambda$.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8505----
