research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8563-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8564-## Facts & Assumptions
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8565-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8566-**Given:** ZF, with no choice principle. Write $n + 1 = n \cup \{n\}$ for the successor of $n \in \omega$ ([[def-natural-numbers]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8567-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log:8568:[L1] The operation $\beta \mapsto \aleph_\beta$ is defined at every ordinal, takes infinite cardinal values, is strictly increasing, satisfies $\beta \le \aleph_\beta$, and is continuous at limits, meaning $\aleph_\lambda = \bigcup\{\aleph_\beta : \beta \in \lambda\}$ for limit $\lambda$ ([[cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined]], [[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8569-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8570-[L2] A class rule $G$ on functions whose domain is an ordinal determines exactly one class function $T$ defined at every ordinal with $T(\beta) = G(T \restriction \beta)$ ([[lem-recursion-on-the-ordinals]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8571-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8572-[L3] Transfinite induction is valid on any well-order, in particular on $(\omega, \in)$ ([[thm-transfinite-induction]], [[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8573-
--
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8603-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8604-**Nothing is chosen, and nothing beyond Replacement is used.** The tower is a definable $\omega$-indexed family, Replacement makes its range a set, and every step of the verification is a computation. So the whole example is a theorem of ZF, like the aleph hierarchy it is built from.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8605-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8606-**Its cofinality is the smallest an infinite cardinal can have.** $\operatorname{cf}(\alpha) = \aleph_0$ says $\alpha$ is reached by an $\omega$-indexed family, which is exactly how it was built. So this fixed point is singular, and by [[cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power]] it is therefore not a possible value of $2^{\aleph_0}$. Nothing above claims it is the least fixed point.
