research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8946-1.2 :: If $\lambda$ is a cardinal with $\kappa < \lambda$ then $\lambda$ does not inject into $\kappa$: an injection $\lambda \to \kappa$ would give $\lambda \le \kappa$ by [L3], contradicting $\kappa \in \lambda$ and trichotomy. [L3, L4]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8947-2.1 :: Claim (a): if $\beta \in \aleph(A)$ had $\beta \approx \aleph(A)$, then composing a bijection $\aleph(A) \to \beta$ with an injection $\beta \to A$ supplied by step 1.1 would inject $\aleph(A)$ into $A$, which step 1.1 forbids; so no element of $\aleph(A)$ is equinumerous with it and $\aleph(A)$ is a cardinal by [L2]. [step 1.1, L2, L5]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8948-2.2 :: First half of claim (b): $\kappa$ injects into $\kappa$ by the identity, so $\kappa \ne \aleph(\kappa)$ by step 1.1; and $\aleph(\kappa) \in \kappa$ is impossible, since then $\aleph(\kappa) \subseteq \kappa$ by [L4] and the inclusion would inject it into $\kappa$; trichotomy leaves $\kappa \in \aleph(\kappa)$, that is $\kappa < \aleph(\kappa)$. [step 1.1, L4, L5]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-8949-3.1 :: Second half of claim (b) and claim (c): a cardinal $\lambda$ with $\kappa < \lambda$ does not inject into $\kappa$ by step 1.2, so $\aleph(\kappa) \le \lambda$ by the minimality in [L1]; with step 2.2 and step 2.1 this makes $\aleph(\kappa)$ the least cardinal strictly above $\kappa$; and if $\omega \le \kappa$ then $\omega \le \kappa < \aleph(\kappa)$ by step 2.2, so $\aleph(\kappa)$ is infinite. [step 1.2, step 2.1, step 2.2, L1, L4, L6] ∎
