research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-9142-L1 => cor-cardinal-absorption,thm-hessenberg :: For an infinite cardinal $\nu$ and a cardinal $\rho \le \nu$: $\nu \oplus \rho = \nu$, and $\nu \otimes \rho = \nu$ when $\rho \ne 0$ ([[cor-cardinal-absorption]], [[thm-hessenberg]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-9143-L2 => lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws :: $\oplus$ and $\otimes$ are commutative and monotone; for cardinals $\kappa \le \lambda$ iff $\kappa \preceq \lambda$; and $A \preceq B$ with both well-orderable gives $\lvert A\rvert \le \lvert B\rvert$ ([[lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log:9144:L3 => def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies,lem-successor-cardinal-exists,cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined :: $\aleph_0 = \omega$; $\aleph_1 = \aleph_0^{+}$ is the least cardinal strictly above $\aleph_0$; every aleph is an infinite cardinal and $\aleph_0 < \aleph_1$ ([[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]], [[lem-successor-cardinal-exists]], [[cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-9145-L4 => thm-cardinal-arithmetic-agrees-with-finite-counting,def-cardinal,def-natural-numbers,lem-omega-least-limit-ordinal :: Every natural number is a cardinal, $\omega$ is a cardinal, and a cardinal is infinite exactly when $\omega \le \kappa$ ([[thm-cardinal-arithmetic-agrees-with-finite-counting]], [[def-cardinal]], [[def-natural-numbers]], [[lem-omega-least-limit-ordinal]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-9146-L5 => thm-n-cross-n-countable,def-countable,def-equinumerous :: $\omega \times \omega \approx \omega$ ([[thm-n-cross-n-countable]], [[def-countable]], [[def-equinumerous]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-9147-L6 => lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set :: $\lvert X\rvert$ is the least ordinal equinumerous with $X$, $X \approx \lvert X\rvert$, and equinumerous sets receive the same one ([[lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-9148-L7 => lem-ordinal-trichotomy,lem-ordinal-basics,def-injection-surjection-bijection :: Ordinals: trichotomy; $\alpha \subseteq \beta$ iff $\alpha \in \beta$ or $\alpha = \beta$; $\alpha \subseteq \beta \subseteq \alpha$ forces $\alpha = \beta$; a subset inclusion is an injection ([[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]], [[lem-ordinal-basics]], [[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-9149-1.1 :: $\aleph_0 = \omega$ and $\aleph_1$ are infinite cardinals with $\aleph_0 \le \aleph_1$, and $5 \in \omega$ is a cardinal with $0 \ne 5 \le \aleph_0$, all by [L3], [L4] and [L7]. [L3, L4, L7]
--
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-9152-1.4 :: The inclusion $5 \sqcup \omega \subseteq \omega \sqcup \omega$ holds because $5 \subseteq \omega$ by [L7], and $n \mapsto (1,n)$ is an injection $\omega \to 5 \sqcup \omega$. [L4, L7]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-9153-2.1 :: By [L1] and [L2]: $\aleph_0 \oplus \aleph_0 = \aleph_0$ and $\aleph_0 \otimes \aleph_0 = \aleph_0$ with $\nu = \rho = \aleph_0$; $\aleph_1 \oplus \aleph_0 = \aleph_1$ with $\nu = \aleph_1$ and $\rho = \aleph_0$; and $5 \oplus \aleph_0 = \aleph_0 \oplus 5 = \aleph_0$ with $\nu = \aleph_0$ and $\rho = 5$. [step 1.1, L1, L2]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-9154-2.2 :: The countable values a second way: step 1.2 gives $\aleph_0 \otimes \aleph_0 = \aleph_0$ outright; step 1.3 with [L2] and [L6] gives $\aleph_0 \le \aleph_0 \oplus \aleph_0 \le \aleph_0 \otimes \aleph_0 = \aleph_0$, hence $\aleph_0 \oplus \aleph_0 = \aleph_0$ by [L7]; and step 1.4 with step 1.3 gives $\aleph_0 \le 5 \oplus \aleph_0 \le \aleph_0 \oplus \aleph_0 = \aleph_0$, hence $5 \oplus \aleph_0 = \aleph_0$. [step 1.2, step 1.3, step 1.4, L2, L6, L7]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-9155-3.1 :: All four values are as displayed, and the three countable ones agree by both routes. [step 2.1, step 2.2] ∎
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-9156-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log:9157:ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum facts=9 steps=7
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-9158-L1 => def-real-numbers,def-complete-ordered-field,def-real-order,thm-of-archimedean,lem-of-q-embeds :: $\mathbb{R}$ is a complete ordered field with a total order ([[def-real-numbers]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-real-order]]), hence Archimedean ([[thm-of-archimedean]]), and $\iota : \mathbb{Q} \to \mathbb{R}$ is the unique order-preserving field embedding ([[lem-of-q-embeds]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-9159-L2 => lem-of-q-dense :: For $x < y$ in an Archimedean ordered field there is a rational $q$ with $x < \iota(q) < y$ ([[lem-of-q-dense]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-9160-L3 => thm-cantor-set-ternary-description,def-cantor-set,def-sequence :: There is a bijection from the set of sequences with values in $\{0,1\}$ onto the Cantor set $C \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ (claim 3 of [[thm-cantor-set-ternary-description]], [[def-cantor-set]]); a sequence of reals is a function $\mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{R}$ ([[def-sequence]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-9161-L4 => thm-rationals-countable,def-countable :: $\mathbb{Q} \approx \mathbb{N}$ ([[thm-rationals-countable]], [[def-countable]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-9162-L5 => lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined :: $A \approx B$ implies $\mathcal{P}(A) \approx \mathcal{P}(B)$ and ${}^{C}A \approx {}^{C}B$ ([[lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-9163-L6 => thm-schroder-bernstein :: If $A \preceq B$ and $B \preceq A$ then $A \approx B$ ([[thm-schroder-bernstein]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-9164-L7 => lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set,def-cardinal,thm-well-ordering-theorem :: $\lvert X\rvert$ is the least ordinal equinumerous with $X$ and equinumerous sets receive the same one ([[lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set]], [[def-cardinal]]); assuming the Axiom of Choice every set has one ([[thm-well-ordering-theorem]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log:9165:L8 => thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor,def-cardinal-arithmetic,def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies :: Assuming the Axiom of Choice, $2^{\kappa} = \lvert \mathcal{P}(\kappa)\rvert$, and $\aleph_0 = \omega$ ([[thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor]], [[def-cardinal-arithmetic]], [[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-9166-L9 => def-injection-surjection-bijection :: A subset inclusion is an injection, a composition of injections is an injection, and a map with a two-sided inverse is a bijection ([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-9167-1.1 :: The two-element sets $2 = \{0,1\}$ and $\{0_{\mathbb{R}}, 1_{\mathbb{R}}\}$ are equinumerous, since $0_{\mathbb{R}} \ne 1_{\mathbb{R}}$ in a field, so ${}^{\omega}2 \approx {}^{\omega}\{0_{\mathbb{R}},1_{\mathbb{R}}\}$ by [L5]. [L1, L5, L9]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-9168-1.2 :: The set ${}^{\omega}\{0_{\mathbb{R}},1_{\mathbb{R}}\}$ is exactly the set of sequences with values in $\{0,1\}$ of [L3], so it is in bijection with $C \subseteq \mathbb{R}$, and composing with the inclusion gives an injection ${}^{\omega}\{0_{\mathbb{R}},1_{\mathbb{R}}\} \to \mathbb{R}$. [L3, L9]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-9169-1.3 :: The map $x \mapsto Q_x := \{\, q \in \mathbb{Q} : \iota(q) < x \,\}$ is an injection $\mathbb{R} \to \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{Q})$: for $x \ne y$ the order is total by [L1], so we may assume $x < y$, and [L2] supplies a rational $q$ with $x < \iota(q) < y$, whence $q \in Q_y$ and $q \notin Q_x$, so $Q_x \ne Q_y$. [L1, L2]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-9170-1.4 :: $\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{Q}) \approx \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})$ by [L4] and [L5]. [L4, L5]
--
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-9172-2.1 :: Chaining steps 1.1, 1.2 gives ${}^{\omega}2 \preceq \mathbb{R}$, and chaining steps 1.3, 1.4, 1.5 gives $\mathbb{R} \preceq {}^{\omega}2$; so [L6] yields $\mathbb{R} \approx {}^{\omega}2$, and with step 1.5 also $\mathbb{R} \approx \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})$, which is clause (a) and uses no choice principle. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 1.3, step 1.4, step 1.5, L6, L9]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-9173-3.1 :: Assuming the Axiom of Choice, all three sets have cardinalities by [L7], equal by step 2.1, and $\lvert \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})\rvert = 2^{\lvert \omega \rvert} = 2^{\aleph_0}$ by [L8] and [L7]; so $\lvert \mathbb{R}\rvert = 2^{\aleph_0} = \lvert \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})\rvert$, which is clause (b). [step 2.1, L7, L8] ∎
