research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-49233-+    },
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-49234-+    {
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-49235-+     "fact": "L10",
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-49236-+     "source": "thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph",
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-49237-+     "source_section": "Statement",
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log:49238:+     "quote": "**(a) In ZF.** Every infinite cardinal $\\kappa$ ([[def-cardinal]]) equals\n$\\aleph_\\alpha$ ([[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]]) for **exactly one** ordinal\n$\\alpha$. So the alephs are not merely a supply of infinite cardinals: they are\nall of them, and the operation $\\alpha \\mapsto \\aleph_\\alpha$ is a bijective\nenumeration of the infinite cardinals by the ordinals.\n\n**(b) Assuming the Axiom of Choice** ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Every infinite\nset is equinumerous ([[def-equinumerous]]) with exactly one aleph.\n\nThe two clauses say different things, and the difference is the whole content of\nthe choice hypothesis. Clause (a) classifies **cardinals**, which are ordinals;\nclause (b) classifies **sets**, and needs to know first that an arbitrary set has\na cardinality at all.",
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-49239-+     "uses": [
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-49240-+      "3.1"
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-49241-+     ]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-49242-+    }
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log-49243-+   ],
