CURRENT TEXT READ: "[L4] For a set $A$ of ordinals, $\bigcup A$ is an ordinal (claim (e) of [[lem-ordinal-basics]]); ordinals satisfy trichotomy; $\alpha \subseteq \beta$ iff $\alpha \in \beta$ or $\alpha = \beta$ ([[lem-ordinal-basics]], [[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]]). Being the least upper bound of $A$ is then immediate from those two clauses: every $\beta \in A$ satisfies $\beta \subseteq \bigcup A$, and any ordinal $\gamma$ with $\beta \subseteq \gamma$ for all $\beta \in A$ satisfies $\bigcup A \subseteq \gamma$." / "[L7] For a well-orderable set $X$, $\lvert X \rvert$ is the least ordinal equinumerous with $X$; and $\lvert \alpha \rvert = \alpha$ exactly when $\alpha$ is a cardinal ([[lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set]], [[def-cardinal]])."
ASSESSMENT: Correct. lem-ordinal-basics says “if A is any set of ordinals then union A is an ordinal” and “alpha is a subset of beta if and only if alpha is an element of beta or alpha equals beta.” lem-ordinal-trichotomy says “exactly one” of the two membership relations or equality holds. Those clauses and the displayed elementary union argument license L4. lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set says “If X is well-orderable there is a least ordinal equinumerous with X” and “the cardinality of alpha equals alpha exactly when alpha is a cardinal.” def-cardinal defines a cardinal as an ordinal equinumerous with no smaller ordinal. This licenses L7. The recursion, cardinal-value, monotonicity, continuity, and fixed lower-bound arguments are correct.
